---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Cube from '../components/Cube.astro';
import { DOC, DOCS_URL, REPO_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'What it is, and what it is not | MetricMine';
const description =
  'MetricMine is a contract-driven data pipeline and a governance loop for AI-assisted modeling. It works alongside dbt, Airbyte, and Claude Code; it is not a replacement for any of them.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/what-it-is/">
  <PageHero eyebrow="What it is" title="What MetricMine is, and what it is not.">
    A fast answer for anyone who arrives from dbt, Airbyte, or a Claude Code
    session and wants to know where this fits. Short version: it sits between
    the tools you already use, and it works alongside them.
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">It is</p>
        <h2>Four true sentences.</h2>
        <ul class="cubes">
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              A contract-driven medallion pipeline that runs end to end on one
              machine: bronze lands raw, a deterministic profiler describes it,
              approved ODCS contracts shape silver and gold, and an
              auto-modeling engine emits the whole gold layer from the approved
              contract.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              A governance loop for AI-assisted modeling. Two narrow agents
              draft contracts. A person approves by merging a pull request.
              Deterministic code and dbt execute. Every approved contract
              records in git who proposed it and which person merged it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              A reference implementation, built in the open, with forty-two
              binding decisions in a versioned register, fifty-seven measured
              findings, and no claim without a reproducible command behind it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              A working demo: seven public sources, three fact categories, one
              star, served read-only over MCP, keyless, from a fresh clone in
              two minutes.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">It is not</p>
        <h2>Six things people ask.</h2>
        <ul class="cubes">
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not a replacement for dbt.</strong> It is a dbt project.
              The transforms are dbt Core models with enforced contracts, and
              the engine emits dbt model files, never DDL. Your dbt skills
              carry over unchanged.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not a replacement for Airbyte.</strong> Bronze lands
              through PyAirbyte. MetricMine starts where the connector stops
              and never touches the source.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not an alternative to Claude Code.</strong> It is a
              repository built to be worked on with Claude Code, and it ships
              the context layer to do it: CLAUDE.md, Oscar the read-only guide
              and reviewer, a review skill, a working-tree guard, and a GitHub
              Action.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not a hosted product.</strong> There is no service, no
              signup, no account, and no pricing. The demo runs keyless on
              your machine, and the only database artifact the project
              publishes is the demo export attached to a release.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not an autonomous agent.</strong> Each of the two agents
              makes one structured API call, holds no warehouse credential,
              writes only to an outbox, and never merges its own work.
              Autonomous multi-step agents are a standing non-goal.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <Cube size={18} />
            <p>
              <strong>Not a promise about scale.</strong> What scale means here
              is measured on stated machines and published with its
              environment in the repository, never as a claim on this site.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Alongside, not instead</p>
        <h2>What it adds to the tools you already run.</h2>
        <p>
          Each row names what stays yours and what MetricMine puts beside it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>If you use</th>
              <th>MetricMine adds</th>
              <th>What stays yours</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>dbt Core</td>
              <td>
                ODCS contracts enforced at build time, generated contract tests
                in CI, an engine-emitted gold layer under an ownership
                manifest, and a scan that adopts an existing model into the
                gates.
              </td>
              <td>Your models, your properties files, your profiles.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Airbyte or PyAirbyte</td>
              <td>
                A landing loop over a declared source list, a committed profile
                per landed table, and a cleanup contract per source that a
                person approves.
              </td>
              <td>Your connectors and their configuration.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>DuckDB</td>
              <td>
                Three schemas in one local file, a read-only serving posture
                three layers deep, and a demo export verified content-equal
                by query.
              </td>
              <td>Your data, on your machine.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Claude Code</td>
              <td>
                Oscar, the resident guide and reviewer, a read-only subagent
                that answers from the repository's own files; CLAUDE.md with
                nineteen hard rules; the contract-review skill; the
                working-tree guard; and the GitHub Action for the authoring
                loop.
              </td>
              <td>Your workflow, plan mode and all.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>An MCP client</td>
              <td>
                A server with five read-only tools over the gold star, and a
                context registry that keeps what was measured apart from what
                an expert wrote.
              </td>
              <td>Your assistant. The demo guide wires Claude Desktop.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The idea in one paragraph</p>
        <h2>The human decides meaning. The machine decides structure.</h2>
        <p>
          The human decides what the data means and how sources reconcile. The
          machine decides how that meaning is structured and served. Meaning is
          declared in two places, the silver cleanup contract and the gold
          mapping contract, and everything downstream of those two declarations
          is a mechanical, byte-reproducible function of them: 22 of the 31 dbt
          models on main are engine-emitted and never hand-written. The nine
          exceptions are silver, the human-owned plane, because silver is where
          meaning is decided. What the agent proposes for silver is the
          contract, not the SQL.
        </p>
        <p>
          That split is what the design buys. A wrong number has an address: it
          traces to the silver logic or to the mapping declaration, never to a
          hand edit somewhere in the DAG. Determinism means the same inputs emit
          byte-identical model files, so a change is reproducible, diffable, and
          reviewable; it does not mean the models are right. Correctness comes
          from separate machinery: contracts enforced at build time, five
          conservation tests in CI, and a grain check that measures a declared
          grain rather than trusting it. The pipeline offers traceability and
          conservation, not observability, and regeneration lands as a pull
          request, never as an automatic update.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            The gold layer is a generic content-addressed container by design,
            not an automated star schema. Its three measured trade-offs are
            recorded where a reader will look, each linked to the finding that
            measured it: <a href={DOC.readme}>the README</a> carries them, and{' '}
            <a href={DOC.goldSpec}>the gold layer spec</a> carries the reading
            rules.
          </p>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <h2>See it answer, or read how it is built.</h2>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/demo/">See the demo</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={DOCS_URL}>Read the docs</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={REPO_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .band {
      background: var(--mm-bg-raised);
      border-block: 1px solid var(--mm-border);
    }

    .split .eyebrow {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
